## 1. Can we force a “nice” query order inside D&C?

Under the usual assumptions:

- DP:
  \[
  \text{dp}[t][i]
  = \min_{0 \le j < i} \{\text{dp}[t-1][j] + \text{cost}(j+1, i)\}
  \]
- `cost(L,R)` = weighted 1D k‑median segment cost with median inside \([L,R]\),
- Standard D&C optimization with monotone optima in \(j\),

then:

- The D&C recursion tree adaptively discovers optima `opt[t][i]`, and
- The set of intervals \((L,R)\) used as `cost(L,R)` calls is:
  - Not known in advance;
  - Interleaved across many subranges of `i`;
  - And their order, as *generated by the recursion*, generally makes large jumps in `L` or `R`.

To combine that with an amortized \(O(1)\) incremental median structure, you would need at least one of:

1. A way to know all relevant \((L,R)\) pairs before evaluation and reorder them globally (Mo-style), or  
2. A way to *constrain* the recursion so that it only walks via \((L,R) \to (L±1,R)\) or \((L,R±1)\) moves.

Both fight directly against what makes D&C viable and elegant:

- The monotone-optima structure is *local* and depends on already computed dp-values.
- The recursion is *inherently adaptive*; you don’t know the child ranges until you know the parent’s optimum.

Any attempt to:
- Make the whole query set explicit *a priori*, or
- Force a global monotone walk order,
essentially re-implements a different algorithm (offline optimization, global DP reformulation, etc.) that loses the simple recursive structure and—practically—ends up more complex than just doing per-interval median in \(O(\log n)\).

So the crisp answer, in the standard toolbox:

**No, there’s no simple, robust way to graft an amortized-constant-time incremental weighted median onto the classic D&C optimization while preserving its structure and guarantees.**  
You either:

- Keep D&C and accept \(O(\log n)\) per `cost(L,R)` via binary search on prefix sums, or
- Drop D&C and design a completely different algorithm that naturally supports a monotone sweep.

