### 2. Minor implementation refinements

Your C++ skeleton is already clear. A couple of tiny nits you might care about in a contest/production setting:

**a) Precompute prefix sums as 1‑based indices**

This slightly cleans the code and may help avoid off‑by‑ones:

```cpp
// sizes: x[1..n], w[1..n]
vector<long long> W(n+1), SW(n+1);

for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    W[i]  = W[i-1]  + w[i];
    SW[i] = SW[i-1] + w[i] * x[i];
}

// sum over [L,R] with 1 <= L <= R <= n
inline long long getSumW(int L, int R)    { return W[R]  - W[L-1]; }
inline long long getSumWX(int L, int R)   { return SW[R] - SW[L-1]; }
```

Then the median code becomes:

```cpp
int findWeightedMedian(int L, int R) {
    long long totalW = getSumW(L, R);
    long long target = (totalW + 1) / 2;

    int lo = L, hi = R, ans = R;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
        long long leftW = getSumW(L, mid);
        if (leftW >= target) {
            ans = mid;
            hi = mid - 1;
        } else {
            lo = mid + 1;
        }
    }
    return ans;
}

long long cost(int L, int R) {
    int m = findWeightedMedian(L, R);
    long long xm = x[m];

    long long WL  = getSumW(L, m);
    long long SWL = getSumWX(L, m);
    long long left  = xm * WL - SWL;

    if (m == R) return left; // common case micro-opt

    long long WR  = getSumW(m+1, R);
    long long SWR = getSumWX(m+1, R);
    long long right = SWR - xm * WR;

    return left + right;
}
```

**b) DP indexing conventions**

If you switch everything to 1‑based for DP too, it can align more nicely with the cost function:

- `dp[t][i]` = best cost to cover points `[1..i]` with `t` groups.
- Transition:

  \[
  \text{dp}[t][i] = \min_{1 \le j \le i} \{\text{dp}[t-1][j-1] + \text{cost}(j, i)\}
  \]

Then your D&C core is essentially:

```cpp
void solveRow(int t, int L, int R, int optL, int optR) {
    if (L > R) return;
    int mid = (L + R) >> 1;

    long long bestVal = LLONG_MAX;
    int bestK = -1;

    int start = optL;
    int end   = min(optR, mid);

    for (int j = start; j <= end; ++j) {
        long long prevCost = dp_prev[j-1]; // j>=1
        long long val = prevCost + cost(j, mid);
        if (val < bestVal) {
            bestVal = val;
            bestK = j;
        }
    }

    dp_cur[mid] = bestVal;

    solveRow(t, L, mid-1, optL, bestK);
    solveRow(t, mid+1, R, bestK, optR);
}
```

Base row:

```cpp
// t = 1
for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    dp_prev[i] = cost(1, i);
}
```

Answer: `dp_prev[n]` after `t = k`.

This avoids special‑casing `j = 0` and empty prefixes.

